<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Clastidium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/222-bce">222 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Clastidium was fought in 222 BCE near Clastidium, modern Casteggio in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and the Insubres with their Gaesatae allies. The consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-claudius-marcellus">Marcus Claudius Marcellus</a> killed the Gallic king Viridomarus in single combat and won the spolia opima, a rare honour. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> then took the Insubrian centre of Mediolanum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-clastidium.jpg" alt="Battle of Clastidium" width="320" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Harris Valda · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Harris_Valda_-_Marcellus_duel_with_Virdumarus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the campaign of the consuls Publius Furius Philus and Gaius Flaminius against them in 223 BCE, the Insubres sent envoys to the Roman senate to ask for peace. The new consuls, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-claudius-marcellus">Marcus Claudius Marcellus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-cornelius-scipio-calvus">Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Calvus</a>, urged that none be granted. Refused, the Insubres hired thirty thousand transalpine Gaesatae as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. The consuls invaded the territory of the Insubres and besieged Acerrae, near modern Pizzighettone between Cremona and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a>. Unable to relieve it, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> crossed the Po into the land of the Anares and besieged the town of Clastidium to draw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> away.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Marcellus hurried to relieve Clastidium with about 3,200 horsemen, two-thirds of his cavalry, and six hundred of his fittest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionaries</a>, while Gnaeus kept up the siege of Acerrae. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> left the siege and drew up for battle. As Marcellus extended his wings to outflank them, his horse panicked and turned away, and he made the moment into a vow to dedicate the finest armour taken from the enemy to Jupiter Feretrius. The Gallic king, called Viridomarus by Florus and Britomartus by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, then rode out and challenged him to single combat. Marcellus unhorsed him with his lance on the first pass, killed him and stripped his armour.</p><p>The Roman cavalry then charged the Gallic horse and foot, attacking from the front and the sides until they broke. Thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> were killed in the pursuit, and many drowned in the Po. It was one of the few Roman battles won by the cavalry without the legions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> soon took Acerrae, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> fell back to Mediolanum. Gnaeus followed, beat off a sally against his rear, laid waste the country and stormed the city, after which the chieftains of the Insubres surrendered unconditionally. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> had conquered the largest independent Celtic tribe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and established its hegemony over the Po Valley, then the most productive farmland of the peninsula.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Clastidium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rome defeated them at Telamon in 225 BCE and at <b>Clastidium</b> in 222 BCE, and took Mediolanum.</blockquote><a href="/state/insubres">Insubres</a></li></ul></section>
